---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'components', label: 'Component testing' },
   { id: 'audits', label: 'Terminal & CI audits' },
   { id: 'agents', label: 'AI coding agents' },
   { id: 'targets', label: 'Execution targets' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Where to start' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Automated screen reader and WCAG testing"
   summary="<p><strong>a11ied</strong> is a CLI, testing library, and MCP server for automated screen reader and WCAG testing.</p><p>Assert what your UI announces to a <abbr title='Assistive technology software that speaks on-screen text and UI controls to blind and low-vision users'>screen reader</abbr> in your Vitest suite, catch <abbr title='Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility'><a href='https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer'>WCAG</a></abbr> violations in CI, and let AI coding agents verify their own fixes.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="components">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Interactive testing</p>
         <h2>Assert what users hear, not just DOM attributes</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Static linters catch missing <code>alt</code> attributes, but they can't tell you if a custom
         combobox traps focus, announces its collapsed state, or reads the active option when a user
         presses ArrowDown. With <code>a11ied/vitest</code>, you can drive a virtual screen reader
         directly inside your test suite—with zero browser setup or operating system permissions.
      </p>
      <pre><code>import {'{'} describe {'}'} from 'vitest';
import {'{'} test {'}'} from 'a11ied/vitest';

const combobox = test.extend({'{'}
   srOptions: {'{'} url: 'http://localhost:3000/components/combobox' {'}'},
{'}'});

describe('combobox component', () => {'{'}
   combobox('announces active option and expanded state', ({'{'} sr {'}'}) => {'{'}
      sr.press('Tab')
         .expect.spoken('combobox, Select country, collapsed')
         .press('ArrowDown')
         .expect.spoken('combobox, Select country, expanded')
         .and.spoken('option, Canada, 1 of 3');
   {'}'});
{'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         Chained commands queue and execute in sequence without littering tests with <code>await</code> boilerplate.
         The in-process virtual screen reader computes the full <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> accessibility tree
         and speech queue in milliseconds, asserting against <strong><abbr title="WCAG 4.1.2: Name, Role, Value requires that UI components have accessible names, roles, and states"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/name-role-value.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)</a></abbr></strong> and <strong><abbr title="WCAG 2.4.3: Focus Order requires that interactive components receive focus in an order that preserves meaning and operability"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-order.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2.4.3 (Focus Order)</a></abbr></strong> as fast as standard unit tests.
      </p>
      <p>
         Building a custom widget from scratch? Query official keyboard shortcuts and expected <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> attributes
         from the <abbr title="W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: official design patterns and keyboard interaction standards for custom web widgets"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG)</a></abbr> directly from your terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 pattern combobox</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="audits">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Auditing & CI</p>
         <h2>Full-page audits with actionable remediation</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Audit local dev servers or staging URLs directly from your terminal or GitHub Actions with
         <code>a1 audit</code>. Powered by <abbr title="Open-source automated accessibility testing engine for HTML and DOM structure from Deque Systems"><a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">axe-core</a></abbr>,
         every failure is mapped directly to its official <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr> success
         criterion, isolated to the offending element, and paired with clear instructions to fix it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>&#10007; 2 problems to fix.

1.1.1  Non-text Content  [A]
  button[type="button"] > img
  Fix: Provide an alt attribute or aria-label for the icon button.

3.3.1  Error Identification  [A]
  input#card-number
  Fix: Reference the error message element using aria-describedby.</code></pre>
      <p>
         Need deeper context on a criterion? Query official <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr> requirements,
         failure scenarios, and test methods without leaving your terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 3.3.1</code></pre>
      <p>
         You can also run <code>a1 inspect</code> to discover which criteria apply to a page before testing
         (see <a href="/relevant-criteria">relevant criteria</a> and <a href="/test-methods">test methods</a>).
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="agents">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agentic workflows</p>
         <h2>A real feedback loop for AI coding agents</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         AI coding assistants generate UI components rapidly, but they have no way to verify whether
         assistive technology actually works with what they write. Without runtime feedback, agents guess
         at <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> attributes
         and frequently introduce accessibility regressions in dynamic interfaces.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>a11ied</code> provides a local <abbr title="Model Context Protocol: open standard for connecting AI coding assistants to external tools"><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</a></abbr> server
         that gives Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, and other agents native tools to inspect accessibility trees,
         drive <abbr title="Apple's built-in screen reader for macOS"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VoiceOver</a></abbr> or <abbr title="NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows"><a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NVDA</a></abbr>,
         and verify their own fixes against <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr> criteria.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{\n  "mcpServers": {\n    "a11ied": {\n      "command": "npx",\n      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]\n    }\n  }\n}`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Give your coding agent tasks that require verifying real interaction, not just inspecting static HTML:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Review the checkout dialog on http://localhost:3000/checkout.
Start a screen reader session, open the modal, and verify:
1. Focus moves to the first field inside the dialog.
2. VoiceOver announces the dialog title and modal role upon opening.
3. Pressing Escape closes the dialog and restores focus to the trigger.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The agent runs keyboard navigation commands, inspects the spoken phrase log, diagnoses failures against
         <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: the W3C standard of testable criteria for digital accessibility"><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WCAG</a></abbr> criteria,
         and iterates on the code until the test passes.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="targets">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Architecture</p>
            <h2>Simulation speed vs. real OS fidelity</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>a11ied</code> provides two distinct execution targets so you can balance instantaneous testing
            speed in CI with full real-world operating system fidelity:
         </p>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>Virtual screen reader</strong>
               <span>
                  A lightweight, headless JavaScript engine that computes the <abbr title="Accessible Rich Internet Applications: W3C specification of attributes that define accessible UI roles, states, and properties"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ARIA</a></abbr> accessibility
                  tree and speech queue in memory. Runs in milliseconds in Node.js or Linux CI containers with zero
                  browser windows, system permissions, or audio hardware required. Ideal for component tests,
                  unit suites, and fast pull request checks.
               </span>
            </div>
            <div class="route-row">
               <strong>VoiceOver & NVDA</strong>
               <span>
                  Real operating system assistive technologies automated via <abbr title="Open-source library for driving VoiceOver and NVDA with code"><a href="https://guidepup.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Guidepup</a></abbr>—using
                  AppleScript on macOS for <abbr title="Apple's built-in screen reader for macOS"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/voiceover/welcome/mac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">VoiceOver</a></abbr> and native accessibility APIs
                  on Windows for <abbr title="NonVisual Desktop Access: the open-source screen reader for Windows"><a href="https://www.nvaccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NVDA</a></abbr>.
                  Ideal for end-to-end integration tests, validating platform-specific speech synthesis, and catching OS quirks before release. See the
                  <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers guide</a>.
               </span>
            </div>
         </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Environment check</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Run <code>a1 doctor</code> in your terminal to check which browsers, permissions, and screen readers
            are configured and ready on your machine.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="feature-block" id="next">
      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <div class="feature-grid">
         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Quickstart</h3>
            <p>Install the package, check your machine, and run your first virtual screen reader session in under two minutes.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/quickstart">Get started</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Component testing</h3>
            <p>Add screen reader assertions to your Vitest suite with the <code>a11ied/vitest</code> fixture, or use <code>a11ied/test</code> with Jest.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/guides/vitest">Vitest guide</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>AI agent setup</h3>
            <p>Connect the MCP server to Cursor, Claude Code, or Windsurf to give your coding assistant accessibility testing tools.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/guides/agents">Agent guide</a></p>
         </section>

         <section class="feature-card">
            <h3>Interactive demo</h3>
            <p>Walk through auditing and fixing real accessibility violations on a sample team settings app.</p>
            <p class="feature-link"><a href="/guides/demo">Practice on demo</a></p>
         </section>
      </div>
   </section>
</DocShell>
